          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Jane\",\"addressee\":\"St John\",\"anchor_quote\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\\nI am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers. With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you; but I am convinced that, go\\nwhen and with whom I would, I should not live long in that climate.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you (as a sister).\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Jane directly avows loving St John, explicitly defining the affection as sisterly. The expression presents her emotional state as true, regardless of its sincerity or St John’s response.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression itself undertakes no action or obligation; it avows sisterly affection. Jane’s surrounding discussion of travelling to India is separate, and she expressly denies being obligated, so no undertaking from that dispute should be transferred onto “I love you.”\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am not under the slightest obligation to go to India\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"Although emotionally meaningful, the expression is used as a deliberate explanatory premise in Jane’s argument—one reason she would have trusted St John—not as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The focal expression’s core force is adequately captured as a truth-presenting avowal of sisterly love. Its contextual role in explaining Jane’s trust does not constitute a distinct residual mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"During an argument about whether Jane has promised or is obliged to accompany St John to India, Jane denies any such obligation. She tells him that she admires, trusts, and loves him as a sister, explaining why she would have risked much specifically with him while still refusing marriage and questioning the journey.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal “I love you” contains no undertaking. In the surrounding exchange Jane distinguishes her sisterly affection from any promise or obligation to travel or marry. St John does not explicitly acknowledge or reciprocate this avowal; his reply instead challenges her fear and later condemns her continuing attachment to Mr Rochester.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as Charlotte Brontë’s Jane Eyre.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence supports classifying the utterance as a sisterly avowal and shows Jane’s stated affection. It does not establish St John’s private interpretation of this exact phrase or any affirmative uptake beyond his subsequent replies.\"}"
        }
      ],
